WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:05.320
So what causes watery slaw?

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:08.680
Well, it turns out, that the problem starts with the cabbage itself.

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:10.240
Vegetables are mostly water.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:11.920
The cabbage is no exception.

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:16.090
In fact, this head of green cabbage is about 93% water.

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:19.957
All that water helps make cabbage crisp and crunchy, which is a good thing.

00:00:19.957 --> 00:00:21.790
But it's, also, water from the cabbage, that

00:00:21.790 --> 00:00:24.630
ends up diluting the dressing in our sad bowl of coleslaw.

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:26.140
It makes such a bland mess.

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:27.910
This is where salt can help.

00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:30.100
The salt slowly diffuses into the vegetable,

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:32.110
but also drawing moisture out.

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:35.410
When salt is applied to any vegetable, including cabbage,

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:38.730
it creates a higher ion concentration at the surface,

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:40.740
than exists deep within the cells.

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:44.200
To equalize the concentration levels, the water within the cells

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:47.120
is drawn out through the permeable cell walls.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:49.360
This process is called osmosis

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:53.530
So the concept of osmosis is very similar to diffusion in a lot of ways,

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:56.960
And it builds on random walks just like diffusion does.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:01.750
And so, just as we have talked about how if you have calcium ions or water

00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:06.880
ions in one part of a solution, they will kind of naturally wander about

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:08.770
and spread out through the solution.

00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:11.470
The same concept kind of applies here, except the difference

00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:18.410
is that in a lot of biological materials and cell walls, there are membranes.

00:01:18.410 --> 00:01:21.270
And they're usually semipermeable as we say.

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:27.010
And semipermeable means that water can diffuse through naturally, whereas,

00:01:27.010 --> 00:01:29.200
other molecules cannot.

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:32.470
And usually, large molecules cannot diffuse through,

00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:36.370
or a very charged molecule, such as ions, cannot diffuse through.

00:01:36.370 --> 00:01:40.200
So here is just a line indicating a cell wall.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:42.850
You can imagine that this is the cell wall of the cabbage.

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:45.520
This cell has water in the inside, and on the outside,

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:47.950
he's going to add salt to the water.

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:50.410
So the water molecules are going to move around randomly,

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:53.380
and they're going to be crossing the membrane in both directions.

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:56.140
But when they find themselves on the side of the membrane that

00:01:56.140 --> 00:01:58.480
has a higher salt concentration, they're going

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:00.680
to have a higher probability of staying there.

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:04.180
And so you're going to have an increase of water flux towards the side

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:06.230
of the higher salt concentration.

00:02:06.230 --> 00:02:08.380
What I'll do is simply toss my shredded cabbage

00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:11.591
with salt. Set it in this colander over this bowl.

00:02:11.591 --> 00:02:13.840
So the salt will pull the moisture out of the cabbage,

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:16.390
and this setup makes sure that the liquid drains away.

00:02:16.390 --> 00:02:19.420
Because we salted the cabbage and carrot ahead of time,

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:23.850
we no longer have to worry about watery coleslaw ever again.

